<Article slug="tea-vessels">
  <p>
    Many of the cups in the shop have no handle. They come from a long Japanese tradition of tea
    vessels, and two names come up again and again: the yunomi and the chawan.
  </p>

  <h2>The yunomi, an everyday cup</h2>
  <p>
    A <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yunomi" rel="noopener">yunomi</a> is a tall,
    handleless cup for everyday tea, usually loose leaf green tea. It is taller than it is wide,
    which keeps tea warm and gives the hand plenty of wall to hold. Because it is a cup for daily
    use rather than ceremony, it is one of the most personal pieces a potter makes: each one is a
    little different, and people tend to have a favourite.
  </p>
  <p>
    Without a handle you feel the warmth of the tea through the clay, and you naturally hold the
    cup with both hands. A faceted or carved wall gives your fingers somewhere to rest.
  </p>

  <h2>The chawan, a bowl for matcha</h2>
  <p>
    A <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chawan" rel="noopener">chawan</a> is a tea bowl, made
    for whisking powdered matcha. It is wide and open so there is room for the bamboo whisk, with a
    deep enough well to froth the tea without splashing. In the
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_tea_ceremony" rel="noopener">tea ceremony</a>,
    guests turn the bowl and admire its surface and foot, so tea bowls are often among the most
    carefully considered pots in a potter's work.
  </p>

  <h2>How to choose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      For daily green tea, coffee or anything sipped slowly, choose a yunomi or handleless cup.
    </li>
    <li>For matcha, choose a wide bowl with a rounded inside that a whisk can move through.</li>
    <li>
      Hold it if you can, or check the size on the listing. A good cup should feel balanced, not
      heavy, when full.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can find both in <a href="/collection/?category=cups-and-tea">Cups and Tea</a>. Every one
    is stoneware fired to around cone 10, so it is food, microwave and dishwasher safe.
  </p>
</Article>
